{"name":"Bitter Leaf","hex":"#669841","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bitter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an acrid taste (usually from a basic substance).","Harsh, piercing, acerbic or stinging."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["To an intense or severe degree; bitterly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bitter"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vernonia amygdalina","blocks":[{"text":"Vernonia amygdalina, a member of the daisy family, is a small to medium-sized shrub that grows in tropical Africa. V. amygdalina typically grows to a height of 2–5 m (6.6–16.4 ft). The leaves are elliptical and up to 20 cm (7.9 in) long. Its bark is rough."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Vernonia_amygdalina_06.jpg/330px-Vernonia_amygdalina_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernonia_amygdalina","wikidata":"Q3744526"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Bitter_leaf_water.jpg/500px-Bitter_leaf_water.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Bitter_leaf_water.jpg/1280px-Bitter_leaf_water.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Bitter_leaf_water.jpg/500px-Bitter_leaf_water.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bitter leaf water in Nigeria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitter_leaf_water.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bitter_leaf_soup_2.jpg/500px-Bitter_leaf_soup_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bitter_leaf_soup_2.jpg/500px-Bitter_leaf_soup_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bitter leaf soup","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitter_leaf_soup_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com